<p>Péter Magyar was sworn in as Hungary’s prime minister on Saturday in parliament in Budapest, ending Viktor Orbán’s 16 years in power. Magyar’s centre-right, pro-European Tisza party won the April 12 parliamentary election, defeating Orbán’s Fidesz and securing a two-thirds majority in the National Assembly. Magyar told lawmakers he would “serve” rather than “rule” Hungary, saying voters had given Tisza a mandate to change not only the government but the system. He has pledged to restore rule-of-law safeguards, strengthen checks and balances and pursue alleged corruption after years of strained relations between Budapest and the European Union. The new government takes office as Hungary seeks access to suspended EU funding to support an economy Reuters described as stagnant and public finances it described as strained. Magyar’s administration is also expected to change Hungary’s role inside the EU, where Orbán often blocked bloc decisions, including measures concerning Ukraine.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/IJ4x4sJZjxTWu16xrMl9Z7uwOP_xnakXJmmW8208rmGnglZ4BH8GQKa9honb9NSA6_6i1RFbCAQ16eCthyGeUbSpJbHi63D_3MfwEIq_9MjuGwIumj9wFEocQcOYecBskdWvKjYoTJEycWRfdDccifGzg4zdVn4Lq2spYAN1gQeo4biBDUFVWj_wSbJb1op8jwXcYL00LHd1uDmhZE3yZLsRdxVJgluVwsGv3R6iFyOc8Ywx6t0NyPLI_spCmPSGP8sG8xReXVawZE4rNA' alt='Hungarian Prime Minister Peter Magyar applauds in front of the Hungarian Parliament after taking his oath of office in Budapest on May 9, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Corruption office: Magyar said he would create an independent office to examine alleged abuses over the past 20 years and recover public assets he says were illegally acquired.</li><li>Fast handover: Tisza moved to elect the prime minister during the new parliament’s first sitting so the government could receive its mandate without delay.</li><li>Public turnout: Tens of thousands of supporters gathered around parliament and watched the ceremony on large screens before Magyar addressed the crowd outside.</li></ul><blockquote>I will not rule over Hungary; I will serve my country. - Péter Magyar</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Péter Magyar: After taking the oath, Magyar described his premiership as a collective rebuilding effort, telling supporters outside parliament that the country “belongs to everyone.” (<a href='https://www.newstalkzb.co.nz/news/world/hungary-s-new-prime-minister-promises-regime-change-and-corruption-probe/'>Newstalk ZB</a>)</li><li>The Kyiv Independent: The outlet said the change in Budapest could reset relations with Ukraine, citing Tisza’s pledge to move away from Orbán’s pro-Moscow direction. (<a href='https://kyivindependent.com/magyar-sworn-in-as-hungarys-pm-as-kyiv-eyes-reset-in-ties/'>Kyiv Independent</a>)</li><li>Courrier International: The French outlet said Hungarian media were asking whether Magyar could prove that “regime change” was more than a slogan and avoid creating major disappointment. (<a href='https://www.courrierinternational.com/article/politique-en-hongrie-peter-magyar-devra-prouver-que-le-changement-de-regime-n-etait-pas-qu-un-slogan_243716'>Courrier International</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/may/09/hungary-prime-minister-peter-magyar-sworn-in-viktor-oban'>Péter Magyar sworn in as Hungary’s prime minister ending 16-year Orbán era</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/peter-magyar-swearing-in-as-hungarys-prime-minister-on-may-9-2026/article70958357.ece'>Magyar sworn in as Hungary’s prime minister on promises of change</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://japantoday.com/category/world/magyar-sworn-in-as-hungary%27s-prime-minister-on-promises-of-change'>Magyar sworn in as Hungary&#x27;s prime minister on promises of change</a> - japantoday.com</li><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/05/10/in-hungary-jubilation-marks-peter-magyar-s-inauguration-as-prime-minister_6753299_4.h